examples.question_text:
Sketch the front, back, right-side and top views of this trapezoidal prism. The front is the nearer trapezium-shaped end. Draw the top view with the prism's length running down the page.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[3,0,0],"C":[2.25,2,0],"D":[0.75,2,0],"E":[0,0,3],"F":[3,0,3],"G":[2.25,2,3],"H":[0.75,2,3]},"faces":[["A","D","C","B"],["E","F","G","H"],["A","B","F","E"],["B","C","G","F"],["C","D","H","G"],["D","A","E","H"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.35],"toward":[0.5,0.35,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (3,0); \coordinate (C) at (2.25,2); \coordinate (D) at (0.75,2);
\coordinate (E) at (1.5,1.05); \coordinate (F) at (4.5,1.05); \coordinate (G) at (3.75,3.05); \coordinate (H) at (2.25,3.05);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(F)--(G)--(H)--(D);
\draw (C)--(G);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(E)--(F); \draw[dashed] (E)--(H);
\draw[->] (1.5,-1)--(1.5,-0.2); \path (1.5,-1) node[below]{Front};
\draw[->] (5.4,1.5)--(4.6,1.5); \path (5.4,1.5) node[right]{Right side};
\draw[->] (2.8,4.1)--(2.8,3.3); \path (2.8,4.1) node[above]{Top};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The front view shows the trapezium-shaped end. The back view is its mirror image; this end face has left-right symmetry, so both views look identical.

The right-side outline is a rectangle. Looking horizontally, the sloping side projects to the prism's full height.

The top outline is a rectangle. Include the two edges where the flat top meets the sloping faces.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\begin{scope}
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(2.25,2)--(0.75,2)--cycle;
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Front};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4.3,0)}]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(2.25,2)--(0.75,2)--cycle;
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Back};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,-4.5)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,2);
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Right side};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4.3,-4.5)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\draw (0.75,0)--(0.75,3); \draw (2.25,0)--(2.25,3);
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Top};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Sketch the front and back views of this prism. Explain how they compare. The front is the nearer end face.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[3,0,0],"C":[3,1,0],"D":[0,2.5,0],"E":[0,0,3],"F":[3,0,3],"G":[3,1,3],"H":[0,2.5,3]},"faces":[["A","D","C","B"],["E","F","G","H"],["A","B","F","E"],["B","C","G","F"],["C","D","H","G"],["D","A","E","H"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.35],"toward":[0.5,0.35,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (3,0); \coordinate (C) at (3,1); \coordinate (D) at (0,2.5);
\coordinate (E) at (1.5,1.05); \coordinate (F) at (4.5,1.05); \coordinate (G) at (4.5,2.05); \coordinate (H) at (1.5,3.55);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(F)--(G)--(H)--(D); \draw (C)--(G);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(E)--(F); \draw[dashed] (E)--(H);
\draw[->] (1.5,-1)--(1.5,-0.2); \path (1.5,-1) node[below]{Front};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The front view has its taller edge on the left. Looking from behind reverses left and right, so the back view has its taller edge on the right.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,1)--(0,2.5)--cycle;
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Front};
\begin{scope}[shift={(4.5,0)}]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,2.5)--(0,1)--cycle;
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Back};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The views are mirror images. They have the same size and shape, but different orientations because the end face has no left-right symmetry.

examples.question_text:
This solid is made from two rectangular prisms joined together. Sketch its front, right-side, left-side and top views. The front is the nearer L-shaped end. Draw the top view with the solid's length running down the page.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[4,0,0],"C":[4,1,0],"D":[1.5,1,0],"E":[1.5,3,0],"F":[0,3,0],"G":[0,0,3],"H":[4,0,3],"I":[4,1,3],"J":[1.5,1,3],"K":[1.5,3,3],"L":[0,3,3]},"faces":[["A","F","E","D","C","B"],["G","H","I","J","K","L"],["A","B","H","G"],["B","C","I","H"],["C","D","J","I"],["D","E","K","J"],["E","F","L","K"],["F","A","G","L"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.35],"toward":[0.5,0.35,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (4,0); \coordinate (C) at (4,1); \coordinate (D) at (1.5,1); \coordinate (E) at (1.5,3); \coordinate (F) at (0,3);
\coordinate (G) at (1.5,1.05); \coordinate (H) at (5.5,1.05); \coordinate (I) at (5.5,2.05); \coordinate (J) at (3,2.05); \coordinate (K) at (3,4.05); \coordinate (L) at (1.5,4.05);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(G)--(H); \draw[dashed] (1.5,3)--(L);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--(E)--(F)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(H)--(I)--(J)--(K)--(L)--(F);
\draw (C)--(I); \draw (D)--(J); \draw (E)--(K);
\draw[->] (2,-1)--(2,-0.2); \path (2,-1) node[below]{Front};
\draw[->] (6.3,2.5)--(5.5,2.5); \path (6.3,2.5) node[right]{Right side};
\draw[->] (2.2,5.1)--(2.2,4.3); \path (2.2,5.1) node[above]{Top};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The front outline is an L-shape.

From the right, the shorter section is in front of the taller section. Draw a rectangular outline and the visible horizontal edge at the shorter section's height.

From the left, the taller section hides the shorter section, so the view is a plain rectangle.

From above, both sections form one rectangular outline. Draw the lengthwise edge where the height changes.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,1)--(1.5,1)--(1.5,3)--(0,3)--cycle;
\path (2,-0.5) node{Front};
\begin{scope}[shift={(5.5,0)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3); \draw (0,1)--(3,1);
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Right side};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,-5)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\path (1.5,-0.5) node{Left side};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(5.5,-5)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (4,3); \draw (1.5,0)--(1.5,3);
\path (2,-0.5) node{Top};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Omit hidden edges from all four flat views.

examples.question_text:
These views show a solid formed from two rectangular prisms. Sketch the solid and label its dimensions. All measurements are in centimetres.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=5mm,y=5mm]
\draw (0,0)--(8,0)--(8,2)--(3,2)--(3,5)--(0,5)--cycle;
\path (4,0) node[below]{$8$}; \path (0,2.5) node[left]{$5$};
\path (1.5,5) node[above]{$3$}; \path (8,1) node[right]{$2$};
\path (4,-1.8) node{Front};
\begin{scope}[shift={(12,0)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,5); \draw (0,2)--(6,2);
\path (3,0) node[below]{$6$}; \path (6,2.5) node[right]{$5$};
\path (3,-1.8) node{Right side};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,-11)}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,6); \draw (3,0)--(3,6);
\path (4,0) node[below]{$8$}; \path (8,3) node[right]{$6$};
\path (1.5,6) node[above]{$3$};
\path (4,-1.8) node{Top};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Draw the L-shaped front face, copy it behind, then join corresponding corners. The side and top views show that the solid extends $6\text{ cm}$ from front to back.

Label the total width $8\text{ cm}$, total height $5\text{ cm}$, taller section's width $3\text{ cm}$ and shorter section's height $2\text{ cm}$.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[8,0,0],"C":[8,2,0],"D":[3,2,0],"E":[3,5,0],"F":[0,5,0],"G":[0,0,6],"H":[8,0,6],"I":[8,2,6],"J":[3,2,6],"K":[3,5,6],"L":[0,5,6]},"faces":[["A","F","E","D","C","B"],["G","H","I","J","K","L"],["A","B","H","G"],["B","C","I","H"],["C","D","J","I"],["D","E","K","J"],["E","F","L","K"],["F","A","G","L"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.35],"toward":[0.5,0.35,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=5mm,y=5mm]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (8,0); \coordinate (C) at (8,2); \coordinate (D) at (3,2); \coordinate (E) at (3,5); \coordinate (F) at (0,5);
\coordinate (G) at (3,2.1); \coordinate (H) at (11,2.1); \coordinate (I) at (11,4.1); \coordinate (J) at (6,4.1); \coordinate (K) at (6,7.1); \coordinate (L) at (3,7.1);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(G)--(H); \draw[dashed] (3,5)--(L);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--(E)--(F)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(H)--(I)--(J)--(K)--(L)--(F);
\draw (C)--(I); \draw (D)--(J); \draw (E)--(K);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,below]{$8\text{ cm}$};
\path (F)--(A) node[midway,left]{$5\text{ cm}$};
\path (E)--(F) node[midway,above]{$3\text{ cm}$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,right]{$2\text{ cm}$};
\path (F)--(L) node[midway,above left]{$6\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The top view has one lengthwise edge where the height changes. The right-side view has one horizontal edge at $2\text{ cm}$ above the base.
